{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The reason focuses on the outcomes of each lottery and considers differences between outcomes as negligible. Here, Lottery A offers 2400 Euros with 100% probability, while Lottery B offers 2500 Euros (33%), 2400 Euros (66%), or 0 Euros (1%). The outcomes of the two lotteries differ by at most 100 Euros (2500 vs. 2400), and there is a small chance (1%) of 0 Euros in Lottery B. The decision reason treats such differences as negligible, so the focus shifts to the probability of a favorable outcome. A 'favorable outcome' under this reason would be any non-zero or acceptable positive outcome—here, likely 2400 or 2500 Euros. Lottery A has a 100% chance of a favorable outcome (2400 Euros). Lottery B has a 99% chance of a favorable outcome (2500 or 2400 Euros) and a 1% chance of 0. Thus, Lottery A has a higher probability of a favorable outcome. The decision reason would therefore prefer Lottery A.",
